# Proposals — the only write channel

The AI's sole mechanism for change: a custom `emit_proposal` tool that
writes **nothing to the filesystem** — it appends a structured line to the
proposal manifest.

## Manifest format (JSONL)

`.ai-proposals/<sessionID>.jsonl` — one JSON object per line:

```json
{
  "ts": 1780000000000,
  "sessionID": "ses_abc123",
  "group": "add-metrics-service",
  "path": "internal/game/game.go",
  "operation": "replace",
  "reason": "Fixed rope tunneling on fast deploy",
  "start_line": null,
  "end_line": null,
  "content": "// full file content",
  "impact": [
    { "path": "internal/physics/physics.go", "symbol": "Substeps", "relation": "reads" }
  ],
  "reuses": [
    { "path": "internal/timefmt/format.go", "symbol": "FormatDuration" }
  ],
  "status": "pending"
}
```

- `impact`: consumers of the changed symbols — **required** when the target
  is a registered shared module (see [17-impact-registry](17-impact-registry.md)).
- `reuses`: canonical implementations the change reuses — proof of no
  duplicated logic.

## Status state machine

```
pending ──da──▶ accepted
pending ──dr──▶ rejected
pending ──gr──▶ needs_revision  ──(AI re-emits)──▶ new pending entry (same group)
```

- `needs_revision`: the user reviewed and the proposal's content/line
  numbers are wrong. The AI is asked to re-read the file and re-emit. The
  NEW proposal is a separate entry in the same group; the old one stays
  marked `needs_revision` (audit trail) until the user deletes it (`dd`).

## The `group` field — multi-file changes as one unit

A single task (e.g. "add the metrics service") touches several files. Each
file gets its own `emit_proposal` call, and every call in the task shares
the same `group` slug. Groups make multi-file changes reviewable and
reversible as a unit.

### Rules

- `group` is a required string — a short slug the AI invents (or the user
  suggests): `add-metrics-service`, `fix-tunneling`, `refactor-input`.
- One group = one logical change across N files.
- Grouping is the ONLY link between proposals — no ordering, no coupling;
  each file is still accepted/rejected independently.

### Why group, not batch

- **Per-file control stays** — you can accept fileA, reject fileB, edit
  fileC. Grouping is for *view* and *revert*, not for surrendering control.
- **Group revert** (see 05): "undo change #42" = restore every snapshot in
  the group, one keypress.
- Groups are auditable: the journal shows which files a change touched.

### Flow for a multi-file task

```
AI task: "add metrics service"
  emit_proposal(fileA, group="add-metrics-service")
  emit_proposal(fileB, group="add-metrics-service")
  emit_proposal(fileC, group="add-metrics-service")
        │
        ▼
picker groups the 3 by group:  "3 proposals: add-metrics-service"
you review each file independently
  accept fileA · edit+accept fileC · reject fileB
        │
        ▼
journal stores the group on every accepted entry
revert-by-group restores all 2 accepted snapshots in one keypress
```

## Impact-aware proposals (M1i)

Touching shared code is no longer a blind proposal. When the target `path`
matches a registered shared module (`.opencode/manage.registry.json`),
`impact` becomes **required**: the model must run `lsp_find_references` on
the symbols it will change and record the real consumers. The plugin
rejects missing, unparseable, or fabricated (non-existent path) impact
lists with a REQUIREMENTS error — a retry, not a terminal denial. See
[17-impact-registry](17-impact-registry.md) for the full semantics.

## Operations

| Operation | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `create` | New file, `content` = full content |
| `replace` | Full-file replacement, `content` = new content |
| `edit_range` | `content` replaces lines `start_line..end_line` |
| `delete` | Remove the file |

## Why tool-call args

Tool call arguments are structured by construction — schema-valid JSON. This
kills the entire "parse the AI's free text" failure mode: the proposal is
never parsed from prose, it IS the structured data.

